{
  "image_name": "1e6445b361844b2fe2e7a47090adecbc.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how two parts of the gut are organized and how different pathogens disturb them. On the left, the small intestine is shown with finger-like villi and deeper crypts beneath a mucus layer. A zoomed schematic suggests that rotavirus mainly affects small-intestinal epithelial cells by altering ion transport and signaling: labels such as NHE3, Ca2+, NSP4 and NSP5 indicate disruption of sodium/proton exchange and intracellular calcium-related processes, which appears to drive abnormal secretion or absorption across the epithelial surface.\n\nIn the middle and right, the colon is shown as a flatter surface with crypts and a mucus covering. The inset for healthy colon highlights junctional and adhesion proteins, including PCDH24, MLPCDH, occludin and tight junctions, suggesting an intact epithelial barrier. Below, enterohemorrhagic E. coli (EHEC) is depicted attaching to the colonic epithelium and delivering EspP, with loss or cleavage of surface and junction-associated components, including a marked reduction of TER. Visually, the main message is that rotavirus and EHEC target different intestinal regions and damage epithelial function in different ways: rotavirus perturbs transport processes in the small intestine, whereas EHEC disrupts barrier integrity in the colon.",
